; Regression test for #3290: `==` in a posting's amount expression sent
; the parser into an endless loop.  Under PARSE_NO_ASSIGN, which posting
; amounts and costs were parsed with, parse_logic_expr rewound the `==`
; token and stayed in its loop, so it read the same token forever.
;
; `==` now compares, so each expression from the issue fails in its own
; way: (1 == 1) and (2 AZN == 1) evaluate to a boolean, which is not an
; amount; (x == 1) names an unknown identifier; and the unclosed
; parenthesis fails to parse.  ledger reports all four.

2025-01-01 * Number
    Expenses:Food    (1 == 1)
    Assets:Cash

2025-01-02 * Identifier
    Expenses:Food    (x == 1)
    Assets:Cash

2025-01-03 * Unclosed parenthesis
    Expenses:Food    (1 + x == 1
    Assets:Cash

2025-01-04 * Commodity
    Expenses:Food    (2 AZN == 1)
    Assets:Cash

test bal -> 4
__ERROR__
While parsing file "$FILE", line 12:
While parsing posting:
  Expenses:Food    (1 == 1)
                   ^^^^^^^^
Error: Amount expressions must result in a simple amount
While parsing file "$FILE", line 16:
While evaluating value expression:
  (x == 1)
   ^
While parsing posting:
  Expenses:Food    (x == 1)
                   ^^^^^^^^
Error: Unknown identifier 'x'
While parsing file "$FILE", line 20:
While parsing posting:
  Expenses:Food    (1 + x == 1
                   ^^^^^^^^^^^
Error: Invalid token '<end of input>' (wanted ')')
While parsing file "$FILE", line 24:
While parsing posting:
  Expenses:Food    (2 AZN == 1)
                   ^^^^^^^^^^^^
Error: Amount expressions must result in a simple amount
end test

; `==` compares in an amount expression, as `!=` already did: 1 == 1
; picks $10.00 and 1 == 2 picks $20.00.  The `=` after each expression
; is still read as a balance assertion, and each assertion holds.

test -f test/regress/3290.dat reg Food
25-Jan-01 Equal                 Expenses:Food                $10.00       $10.00
25-Jan-02 Unequal               Expenses:Food                $20.00       $30.00
end test

; It compares in a cost expression too: 2 == 2 picks the $50.00 per-unit
; cost, so ten shares cost $500.00.

test -f test/regress/3290.dat reg Brokerage --basis
25-Jan-03 Broker                Assets:Brokerage            $500.00      $500.00
end test
